{
  "decision": "revise",
  "headline": "Dated slip check lowers NVIDIA base to $371.43bn versus $376bn street; Q3 now $102bn",
  "note": "Supply Chain Signals now alleges $12–15bn of calendar-Q4 deliveries moving to Q1 2027, roughly 70% NVIDIA systems. Two ODMs report customer requests and two power vendors report campus delays [channel-q4-slip]. This is more specific than the earlier general warning, but both checks use the same vendor and unnamed respondents; overlap and matched order confirmation remain unresolved [channel-oct2026]. Searches and refreshed SEC indices supplied no independent dated confirmation or customer identities.\n\nThe NVIDIA-branded portion is $8.4–10.5bn of system value, not recognized revenue. I size a roughly $3.2bn base revenue allowance using the $13.5bn midpoint, 70% NVIDIA, assumed two-thirds supplier content and a 50% net verification/recognition weight. Those conversion assumptions are my judgment. NVIDIA’s control-transfer policy and customer advances prevent treating the planner’s payment rule as universal [web-b93e425ae9] [web-81e32d74b6].\n\nI lower effective accelerator realization and cohort concentration together, preserving approximately unchanged revenue outside the modeled four. Company capex budgets remain $682.5bn pending identities, payment measures and possible offsets; actual cash deferral is unestablished. AMD stays $14.00bn through calibration, not a share-gain claim.\n\nNVIDIA base becomes $371.43bn. I revise Q3 Data Center $103bn to $102bn and own fiscal Q4 $128bn to $127bn, assuming roughly even old October–December/new January–March placement. January remains fiscal Q4. Orders are reportedly deferred, preserving demand rather than adding new 2027 demand.\n\nNVIDIA’s long calendar earnings case weakens: forecast falls $3.19bn, widening the street deficit from $1.38bn to $4.57bn (-1.22%).",
  "stance": "Current-year accelerator realization weakens despite larger forward orders; NVIDIA falls further below street, while AMD stays near consensus.",
  "rationale": "The new report adds dated requests and system-dollar indications, legitimately changing timing odds despite unverified independence and identities. Apply a partial direct revenue allowance rather than equating branded systems with supplier sales. Joint mix/concentration changes avoid grossing a cohort-specific loss up into unaffected revenue; AMD base is recalibrated to its disclosure-limited street anchor. Monthly allocation judgments lower both upcoming NVIDIA fiscal-quarter estimates $1bn. Hold customer capex until cash/lease/offsets can be attributed; the realization adjustment is a provisional model proxy, not confirmed cash accounting.",
  "thesis": "Calendar 2026 capex budgets remain $682.5bn versus $667.5bn capex street. NVIDIA Data Center bear/base/bull is now $343.79bn/$371.43bn/$391.79bn versus $376bn consensus; base falls $3.19bn (-0.85%) and is $4.57bn below street (-1.22%). Base accelerator realization is 37.3%, NVIDIA spend share 65%, modeled-four concentration 44.55%; bear realization/concentration 35.3%/44.85%. AMD base split recalibrates to 2.75% to retain approximately $14bn GPU revenue; other AMD drivers and bull are held. Reported Q4-to-Q1 system deferrals are not independently verified and actual customer cash/supplier recognition remain unresolved. Unreported NVIDIA Q3 FY27 Data Center is revised to $102bn, expected November 18 unconfirmed; own Q4 to $127bn versus $126.58bn required. January receipts remain fiscal Q4, so calendar and fiscal impacts differ.",
